A place that leaves the spare or spent queue drops the core's release while another still waits on the other.
The kernel would not look for that one, and it would wait past the count its virtual time came within a span of;
a core owes no release only while nobody waits on its spare and spent queues.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: a lender's virtual time comes before the release
Report: a place's virtual time comes before the release
Witness: fuzz lend-own-time-comes

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -159,7 +159,7 @@ static void place_unqueue(struct place *p)
     struct core *core = &cores[p->core];
     ring_remove(queue_head(core, p->queue), v2p(p), QUEUED);
     p->queue = QUEUE_NONE;
-    if (core->spare_queue == 0 && core->spent_queue == 0) {
+    if (core->spare_queue == 0 || core->spent_queue == 0) {
         core->nearest_release = COUNT_NEVER;
     }
 }
